WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 1.4: Post-Installation Configuration. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 1.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.050
4, Post-Installation Configuration.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.500
In Topic 1.

4
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.750
3 you installed two servers.

5
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
They work, but they are not ready for real
jobs.

6
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.050
They have random names, no fixed address,
and the wrong time zone.

7
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:22.300
In this video you will plan names and
addresses, and you will learn why the

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:23.750
clock matters.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.600
You will set everything up on SRV01 with
Server Manager, and on SRV-CORE with

10
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.050
SConfig.

11
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.300
You will also see the PowerShell command
for each task.

12
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.750
Finally, you will check updates,
activation and connectivity.

13
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.200
Here is the checklist for every new
server.

14
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:46.050
First, give it a name that follows your
plan.

15
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.700
Second, set a static IP address.

16
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.550
Third, set the time zone and check the
clock.

17
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.400
Fourth, turn on Remote Desktop so admins
can work on it from another computer.

18
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.650
Fifth, decide how updates arrive.

19
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.300
Sixth, check that Windows is activated.

20
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.350
And seventh, restart so every change is
applied, then verify your work.

21
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.200
In the lab you follow it twice: in the GUI
and on Server Core.

22
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.450
First, write down the plan.

23
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.900
SRV01 gets 192.

24
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.350
168.

25
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.800
50.

26
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.050
20, and SRV-CORE gets 192.

27
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.500
168.

28
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.950
50.

29
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.400
21.

30
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.050
Both use the subnet mask 255.

31
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.500
255.

32
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.950
255.

33
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.400
0, which is the same as slash 24.

34
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:43.050
The pod has no router, so there is no
default gateway, and DNS stays empty until

35
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.500
Learning Plan 3.

36
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.750
Names use letters, numbers and hyphens,
fifteen characters at most.

37
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.800
Right now each server shows a 169.

38
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.250
254 address.

39
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.900
That is APIPA: Windows picks it when no
DHCP server answers.

40
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:02.550
Servers need a fixed address, so others
can always find them.

41
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.400
Why fuss about time?

42
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:09.450
Windows stores time in UTC and shows it in
your time zone.

43
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.100
Our pods use Central Standard Time.

44
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.550
The bigger reason comes in Learning Plan
4.

45
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.600
When you sign in with a domain account,
Kerberos checks the time.

46
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:26.850
By default, if a computer's clock is more
than five minutes away from the domain

47
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.300
controller, sign-in fails.

48
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.550
Time also matters for logs.

49
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.200
When servers agree on the time, you can
line up their events and see what happened

50
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:38.650
first.

51
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.700
On a Desktop Experience server, Server
Manager opens when you sign in.

52
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.350
Select Local Server on the left.

53
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.200
The Properties tile lists the settings
from our checklist.

54
00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:55.250
The trick is that each value on the right
is a link.

55
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.900
Select the value next to Computer name to
rename the server.

56
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.350
The Remote Desktop value opens the Remote
tab.

57
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.200
Ethernet opens Network Connections.

58
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.850
Windows Update opens the update settings.

59
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.500
Time zone opens Date and Time.

60
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.950
And Product ID shows whether Windows is
activated.

61
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.800
One page, six tasks.

62
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.650
When you select the computer name, System
Properties opens.

63
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.700
Select Change, and you get the Computer
Name slash Domain Changes dialog.

64
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.950
Type the new name, SRV01.

65
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.200
Leave the Workgroup setting as it is,
because we join a domain later in the

66
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.650
course.

67
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.100
Select OK.

68
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.150
Windows tells you that you must restart.

69
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.200
Select OK, then Close, then Restart Now.

70
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.650
Why restart?

71
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:49.900
The name is saved right away, but Windows
starts using it only after a restart.

72
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.150
Until then, Server Manager still shows the
old WIN name.

73
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.600
Next, the address.

74
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:00.250
Select the Ethernet link, right-click the
Ethernet adapter, and choose Properties.

75
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.300
Select Internet Protocol Version 4, then
Properties.

76
00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.350
Now choose Use the following IP address.

77
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:07.800
Type 192.

78
00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:09.250
168.

79
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.700
50.

80
00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.150
20 for SRV01.

81
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.800
When you select the Subnet mask box,
Windows fills in 255.

82
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.250
255.

83
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.700
255.

84
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.150
0 for you.

85
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.400
Leave Default gateway empty, because the
pod has no router.

86
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.050
Windows also switches DNS to Use the
following DNS server addresses.

87
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.900
Leave both of the DNS boxes empty for now.

88
00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.550
Then select OK, and then Close.

89
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.000
Two more dialogs.

90
00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.450
The Time zone link opens Date and Time.

91
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.700
Select Change time zone, and pick Central
Time, US and Canada, the one marked UTC

92
00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.150
minus six.

93
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.000
Keep daylight saving checked.

94
00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:56.050
For Remote Desktop, the link opens the
Remote tab of System Properties.

95
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.100
Select Allow remote connections to this
computer.

96
00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.150
Keep the Network Level Authentication box
checked.

97
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.000
With it, people must sign in before a
session even starts, which is safer.

98
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:12.250
Turning Remote Desktop on here also opens
its firewall rule.

99
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.300
Server Core has no Server Manager window.

100
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.350
Instead, when you sign in, SConfig opens.

101
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.600
It is a text menu.

102
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.650
You type a number and press Enter.

103
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.100
The numbers you need are on the right.

104
00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.350
Two is the computer name.

105
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.200
Eight is network settings.

106
00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.450
Nine is date and time.

107
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.300
Seven is Remote Desktop.

108
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.150
Five is the update setting, and six
installs updates.

109
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.600
Eleven shows activation.

110
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:44.450
Thirteen restarts the server, and fifteen
exits to PowerShell.

111
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.500
If you leave SConfig, type SConfig and
press Enter to come back.

112
00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:51.350
On SRV-CORE, type eight.

113
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.600
SConfig lists your network adapters.

114
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.050
Pick index one.

115
00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.100
Then choose one, Set network adapter
address.

116
00:05:58.350 --> 00:05:59.950
Type S for static.

117
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.400
Type 192.

118
00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:02.850
168.

119
00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.300
50.

120
00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.950
21, then the subnet mask, 255.

121
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.400
255.

122
00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:09.850
255.

123
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.300
0.

124
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:16.750
When it asks for a default gateway, leave
it blank and press Enter.

125
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.600
Skip DNS for now.

126
00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:24.850
One warning: on a server with an APIPA
address, SConfig can fail with error 83,

127
00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.900
because it cannot release a DHCP lease.

128
00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.550
If that happens, go to PowerShell.

129
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.000
Turn DHCP off with Set-NetIPInterface,
remove the 169.

130
00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.250
254 address, then run New-NetIPAddress.

131
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.500
Every task you just saw also has a
PowerShell command.

132
00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:42.750
Get-TimeZone shows the current zone.

133
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Setup often leaves Pacific Time.

134
00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:50.850
To change it, type Set-TimeZone, dash Id,
and the Id in quotes: Central Standard

135
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.300
Time.

136
00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:56.150
The quotes are needed because the Id has
spaces.

137
00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.400
To check, put Get-TimeZone in brackets and
add dot Id.

138
00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:03.450
Last, Rename-Computer, dash NewName
SRV-CORE, dash Restart.

139
00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.300
Do the rename last.

140
00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:10.750
The dash Restart switch restarts the
server right away, so anything after it

141
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.600
would have to wait.

142
00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.850
For the address, first find the adapter
name with Get-NetAdapter.

143
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.900
Here it is Ethernet, but use the name your
own pod shows.

144
00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:25.750
Next, turn DHCP off with
Set-NetIPInterface, dash Dhcp Disabled.

145
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.800
Then run New-NetIPAddress with the
interface alias, the IP address, and dash

146
00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.250
PrefixLength 24.

147
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.500
Prefix length 24 means 255.

148
00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:35.950
255.

149
00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.400
255.

150
00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:38.850
0.

151
00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:43.100
There is no gateway, because our pod has
no router.

152
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.750
Get-NetIPConfiguration then shows the
final result.

153
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.600
The IPv4 address is set, and the default
gateway is empty, just as planned.

154
00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.650
Remote Desktop in PowerShell takes two
steps.

155
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.500
First, set a registry value called
fDenyTSConnections to zero.

156
00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.150
That means deny connections is off.

157
00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.400
Second, enable the firewall rules in the
Remote Desktop group.

158
00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.250
The GUI and SConfig do both steps for you.

159
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.700
Now ping.

160
00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.350
Windows Server blocks it by default.

161
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.200
To allow it, enable the rule named File
and Printer Sharing, Echo Request, ICMPv4

162
00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:20.650
In.

163
00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.500
We store the long name in a variable
first.

164
00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:29.150
On Server Core, SConfig option four, then
three, does the same.

165
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:35.400
Our pods have no internet, so you check
updates and activation, but you do not

166
00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:36.850
change them.

167
00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:41.500
In Server Manager, use the Windows Update
and Product ID links.

168
00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:47.350
In SConfig, option five shows the update
setting: A for automatic, D for download

169
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:49.600
only, and M for manual.

170
00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.250
Option eleven, then one, shows activation.

171
00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:57.300
Companies with servers off the internet
use WSUS, Windows Server Update Services.

172
00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:02.350
One server downloads updates, admins
approve them, and the others install them.

173
00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.400
For activation, they use KMS or AVMA.

174
00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.250
Now prove it works.

175
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:13.100
On SRV01, ipconfig slash all shows the
host name, DHCP Enabled No, the new

176
00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.950
address and the mask, and an empty default
gateway.

177
00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.400
Then run Test-Connection to SRV-CORE with
dash Quiet, which prints just True or

178
00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:23.850
False.

179
00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.500
The first time you get False.

180
00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:27.950
Don't panic.

181
00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.800
The network is fine.

182
00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:36.450
Windows Server turns off the echo request
rule by default, and our pod counts as a

183
00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:37.900
public network.

184
00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:43.350
Turn on response to ping on SRV-CORE, test
again, and you get True.

185
00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:44.800
Four common mistakes.

186
00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:48.250
One: renaming the server and skipping the
restart.

187
00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:52.100
The new name only takes effect after a
restart.

188
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:57.150
Two: typing a gateway or DNS server just
to fill the box.

189
00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:02.600
Leave them empty, because a wrong address
causes slow lookups and strange errors

190
00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:04.050
later.

191
00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.900
Three: deciding the network is broken
because ping fails.

192
00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:10.550
Remember, ping is blocked by default.

193
00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.600
Four: giving two servers the same address.

194
00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:19.850
Follow the plan, SRV01 is dot twenty and
SRV-CORE is dot twenty-one, and check with

195
00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.300
ipconfig.

196
00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.150
Time to check yourself.

197
00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.800
A classmate is setting up SRV-CORE.

198
00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.850
First, they renamed it in SConfig and said
no to the restart.

199
00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.900
What name is it using right now?

200
00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:38.950
Second, which SConfig options set the
static address and the time zone?

201
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:43.600
Third, ipconfig on SRV-CORE shows the
right address, but Test-Connection from

202
00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.050
SRV01 says False.

203
00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.700
What is the most likely cause?

204
00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:53.550
And fourth, why will a wrong clock matter
more once we have a domain?

205
00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:57.400
Pause the video, think about your answers,
then continue.

206
00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.250
Here are the answers.

207
00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.700
One: it still uses the old WIN name.

208
00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:08.550
The new name takes effect only after a
restart, which is option thirteen in

209
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.000
SConfig.

210
00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:15.450
Two: option eight sets the network
address, and option nine opens the Date

211
00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.500
and Time dialog for the time zone.

212
00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.150
Three: SRV-CORE blocks ping by default.

213
00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:25.800
Use option four, then three, to allow it,
and test again.

214
00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:31.650
Four: domain sign-in uses Kerberos, and
Kerberos rejects a clock that is more than

215
00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.100
five minutes off.

216
00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.350
SConfig is open on SRV-CORE.

217
00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.400
I type eight and pick adapter one.

218
00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:39.850
See the 169.

219
00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:41.300
254 address?

220
00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:45.550
I choose one, then S for static, and type
192.

221
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.000
168.

222
00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:48.450
50.

223
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.300
21 and the mask.

224
00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:52.950
For the gateway I press Enter.

225
00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.400
Now nine.

226
00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:58.650
The Date and Time dialog opens, even on
Server Core.

227
00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.700
I pick Central and select OK twice.

228
00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:05.950
Seven, E and one turn on Remote Desktop
with NLA.

229
00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.200
Four and three allow ping.

230
00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:09.650
Fifteen opens PowerShell.

231
00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:14.300
Get-TimeZone dot Id says Central Standard
Time, and Get-NetIPConfiguration shows the

232
00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:15.750
address.

233
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.400
I type SConfig to go back.

234
00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:22.650
Last, option two, the name SRV-CORE, and Y
to restart.

235
00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:25.700
After the restart, the menu shows
SRV-CORE.

236
00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.950
To wrap up: plan first.

237
00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:33.400
A name, a static address, a mask, and no
gateway in our pod.

238
00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:38.450
On Desktop Experience, Server Manager's
Local Server page is your starting point.

239
00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:44.300
On Server Core, SConfig does the same with
numbers: two, eight, nine and seven.

240
00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.350
PowerShell can do all of it too.

241
00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:48.800
In Lab 1.

242
00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:54.650
4 you set up both servers, check updates
and activation, test the connection, and

243
00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.100
record your settings.

244
00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:59.150
It takes about fifty minutes, practice
only.

245
00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.400
See you in the lab.
